solution.p:
Differentiating the left side needs both chain rule and product rule:
\begin{aligned}
\frac{d}{dx} e^{x^2 y} \amp = e^{x^2 y} \cdot \left( \frac{d}{dx} x^2 y\right)\\
\amp = e^{x^2 y} \left({2x} + x^2 \frac{dy}{dx}\right)
\end{aligned}

Solving for \displaystyle{\frac{dy}{dx}} gives
\begin{aligned}
{2x}e^{x^2 y} + x^2e^{x^2 y} \frac{dy}{dx} \amp = 10+4 \frac{dy}{dx}\\
\left(x^2e^{x^2 y} - 4\right) \frac{dy}{dx} \amp = 10 - {2x}e^{x^2 y}\\
\frac{dy}{dx} \amp = \frac{10 - {2x}e^{x^2 y}}{x^2e^{x^2 y} - 4}
\end{aligned}
